{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological evolution has no conscious agent making a 'decision', whereas Popper's scientist actively chooses.","Chess openings rely on opponent psychology and established patterns (inductive elements), whereas Popperian science explicitly rejects pattern-based prediction."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that choosing the 'sharpest' theory inherently requires a probabilistic judgment about the stability of the testing environment.","If the distinction between 'methodological convention' and 'inductive belief' proves to be merely verbal with no practical difference in action."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on past survival/performance without assuming future repetition of conditions.","Preference for the option that exposes the agent to the highest resolution feedback (falsification/checkmate).","Decision rule is procedural/strategic rather than probabilistic/predictive."],"source_memory_refs":["evolutionary_selection_of_phenotypes","chess_opening_preparation"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a chess player chooses a sharp opening to force a decisive result quickly (win or lose) rather than a drawish line, a scientist chooses a highly corroborated, high-content theory to force a quick falsification or temporary survival.","Evolution selects traits that survived past environments not because the future will match the past, but because those traits represent the only existing data points of success; similarly, science selects theories that survived tests as the only available tools for further testing."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms not as an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past, but as a non-inductive 'conventionalist decision' to act on the theory that has withstood the most severe tests, thereby maximizing the objective chance of learning from error (falsification) rather than maximizing subjective probability of truth.","counterconditions":["If the agent believes the preferred theory is more likely to be true in the future based solely on past survival, they have smuggled in induction.","If the preference relies on a hidden assumption that the laws of nature are stable over time (uniformity), the defense collapses into the very metaphysical faith Popper rejected.","If the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) is required to justify the preference, the defense fails because Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude was logically untenable."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reframes the scientist's preference from a predictive bet (which requires induction) to a methodological rule for selecting the most informative potential falsifier. By choosing the theory with higher content and surviving tests, one selects the hypothesis that offers the greatest opportunity for future refutation. If it survives, we learn nothing new; if it fails, we learn significantly. This aligns with Popper's goal of rapid error elimination without assuming nature's uniformity.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}